---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const vtCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: 'F*x/m', label: 'v(t) = (F/m)·t', color: '#1d4ed8' }],
  params: [
    { name: 'F', label: 'pushing force F', min: 0, max: 50, step: 1, value: 10, unit: 'N' },
    { name: 'm', label: 'mass m', min: 1, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 2, unit: 'kg' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 't (s)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const labCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'F', label: 'force F', min: 0, max: 50, step: 1, value: 10, unit: 'N' },
    { name: 'm', label: 'mass m', min: 1, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 2, unit: 'kg' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'acceleration a = F/m', expr: 'F/m', unit: 'm/s²' },
    { label: 'force needed for 5 m/s²', expr: '5*m', unit: 'N' },
    { label: 'weight on Earth (m·g)', expr: 'm*9.81', unit: 'N' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/foundational/newtons-laws">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1687 Isaac Newton published the <em>Principia</em> and, in three sentences, explained
    almost every push, pull and crash you will ever see. <strong>First law:</strong> things keep
    doing what they're doing — sitting still or moving straight at constant speed — unless a net
    force intervenes. That stubbornness is <em>inertia</em>, and it's why you lurch when a bus
    brakes. <strong>Second law:</strong> force equals mass times acceleration. <strong>Third
    law:</strong> forces always come in pairs — you push the wall, the wall pushes you back
    equally.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="velocity grows in proportion to force and inversely to mass — the second law, plotted" widget="plotter" config={vtCfg} />

  <p>
    Drag the force and mass sliders: the same push accelerates a truck far less than a bicycle.
    Double the mass and you halve the acceleration — that ratio <code>F/m</code> <em>is</em> the
    second law.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the second law as a calculator — plus what your mass weighs on Earth" widget="sliderLab" config={labCfg} />

  <h2>Reading the three laws</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>First law (inertia).</strong> No net force → no change in velocity. Seatbelts exist because <em>you</em> keep moving when the car stops.</li>
    <li><strong>Second law (dynamics).</strong> a = F/m. The unit of force, the newton (N), is defined by it: 1 N accelerates 1 kg at 1 m/s².</li>
    <li><strong>Third law (action–reaction).</strong> Rockets work in empty space by throwing gas backward; the gas throws the rocket forward. The two forces act on <em>different</em> objects — which is why they never cancel.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Notice the first law is really the second with F = 0. And "mass" appears in two roles: inertia
    (resisting acceleration) and weight (feeling gravity) — an eerie coincidence that finally made
    sense in general relativity, two centuries later.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Newton, Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica (1687); OpenStax, College Physics.</p>
</TopicPage>
